bw-engine — provenance and third-party references
==================================================

This file describes where the knowledge and code in this repository came
from. It is a good-faith description, not legal advice.

replay-core (replay parser) — crates/replay-core
-------------------------------------------------
Written independently for this project. The replay container format,
command payload lengths, and Remastered section layout (SKIN, LMTS, BFIX,
CCLR, GCFG) were cross-checked against the publicly documented behaviour
of:

  * screp (https://github.com/icza/screp), Apache License 2.0
  * BWAPI (https://github.com/bwapi/bwapi), LGPL-3.0

Only factual format details (byte offsets, field sizes, opcode lengths)
were used; no source code was copied. Custom player colours from CCLR are
decoded directly from their float RGBA encoding rather than via screp's
colour lookup table.

bw-engine (simulation and asset parsing) — crates/bw-engine
-----------------------------------------------------------
The simulation (game loop, movement, pathfinding, combat, vision) and parts
of the map loader were written by studying OpenBW's `bwgame.h`
(https://github.com/OpenBW/openbw; mirror at
https://github.com/broodwar-live/openbw). Source comments cite the
`bwgame.h` line numbers that were used as the behavioural reference.

Most of the engine is an independent Rust design that reproduces BW
behaviour. However, some parts follow OpenBW closely enough to be
considered derived from it:

  * crates/bw-engine/src/direction.rs — the 256-entry direction unit
    vector table is the same data as OpenBW's `direction_table`.
  * crates/bw-engine/src/map.rs — mega-tile flag summarisation
    (walkable/height thresholds) and border-tile flag rules mirror
    `bwgame.h` closely, statement for statement.
  * crates/bw-engine/src/tileset.rs, chk.rs, game.rs (starting-unit
    order), fp8.rs, unit.rs — constants, ordering and fixed-point
    conventions matched against OpenBW.

The values in these tables and thresholds originate from StarCraft: Brood
War itself (OpenBW reverse-engineered them); they are functional game
constants rather than creative expression, but they were obtained via
OpenBW.

As of this writing, the OpenBW/openbw repository does not include a
licence file or licence headers. OpenBW's BWAPI fork
(https://github.com/OpenBW/bwapi) is LGPL-3.0. Without an explicit licence
from the OpenBW authors, the MIT licence declared for this workspace cannot
be assumed to cover the OpenBW-derived portions of crates/bw-engine listed
above. The replay parser (replay-core) and its bindings do not depend on
them; a parser-only build (`replay-wasm --no-default-features`,
`replay-nif`) excludes the simulation entirely.

